### 1.5 Planned Learning Resources and Mini Lecture

The project produces three connected learning resources – the study guide and test bank, the PPT slides, and the video tutorials – built on the shared Markdown content, plus the live mini lecture. The shared Markdown content is the detailed foundation and the main source for all other materials, so that the information stays consistent (Table 3).

**Table 3. Planned learning resources, their purpose, and the expected outcome.**


| Resource | Purpose | Expected outcome |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Shared Markdown Content | The detailed foundation of the project. It organizes explanations across the six chapters in learning order and includes definitions, key concepts, examples, activities, and references. It is the main source for the other materials. | Six approved chapter files in the repository (one per chapter lead), reviewed by the Quality Lead and the Content Lead. |
| 2. Study Guide and Test Bank | The study guide helps learners study each chapter independently: objectives, explanation, worked example, practice exercises, and explained answers, with references. The test bank helps learners practise and check their understanding with questions on how software processes information, identifying facts and rules, understanding rule-based systems, tracing a forward-chaining process, and how software and symbolic AI work together. | One study guide covering all six chapters, a test bank with an answer key, tested on sample questions before Stage 2. |
| 3. PPT Slides | The slides present the main ideas from the chapters concisely and visually, including diagrams of how software processes information, symbolic AI and knowledge representation, and the parts of a rule-based system, along with a step-by-step forward-chaining example. | One slide deck used in the live mini lecture, with the knowledge base / inference engine diagram and the library-loan walkthrough. |
| 4. Video Tutorials | Short tutorials with narration and visual demonstrations: how software processes information, how symbolic AI represents knowledge, and how a rule-based system applies rules to reach a conclusion. Step-by-step visuals help learners follow processes such as forward chaining. | Narrated video tutorial(s) with subtitles and visuals, built from the approved video script. |
| 5. Live Mini Lecture | A focused explanation of one idea – how a rule-based system uses facts and IF–THEN rules to reach a conclusion – with an audience activity (plan in Table 4). | Mini lecture delivered according to the class schedule; learners trace a new case and state the conclusion. |

**How the resources support one another.** The Markdown content provides the detailed explanations and references. The slides and videos present selected ideas visually, while the study guide and test bank help learners review and practise them. The mini lecture brings one part of the project to life through a focused explanation and audience activity. All resources use consistent terms while drawing on examples from different topics.

**Mini lecture plan.** The proposed topic from the topic menu is rule-based reasoning; the mini lecture focuses on one important idea: how a rule-based system uses facts and IF–THEN rules to reach a conclusion. This idea matters because it is the simplest complete example of software reaching a conclusion from explicit knowledge, and the rest of the module – expert systems, rule engines, strengths and limitations – builds on it. One scenario is used throughout – the library loan – so that learners follow a single example from facts to conclusion. The facts are: the member has a valid library card; the member has no overdue books; the requested book is available. The rules are: R1: IF the member has a valid card AND has no overdue books THEN the member may borrow; R2: IF the member may borrow AND the book is available THEN the loan is approved. Forward chaining applies R1 to add the new fact “the member may borrow”, then R2 to reach the conclusion “the loan is approved”. The segments, presenters, and timing are given in Table 4; the timing is scaled to the slot allowed by the class schedule.

**Table 4. Mini lecture plan – segments, teaching approach, presenter, and indicative time.**


| Segment | Teaching approach | Presenter | Time |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1. Opening and scenario | Pose the focus question and introduce the library-loan scenario as a familiar decision; state the three facts. | Gunawan Nicolas Benedictus | 2 min |
| 2. Key terms | Define fact, condition, IF–THEN rule, and AND using the scenario’s own facts; learners spot the conditions in R1. | Ritchard Alexander | 3 min |
| 3. Parts of the system | Show the knowledge base (facts + R1, R2) and the inference engine on one diagram and explain what each part does. | Louis Alexander Dalim | 3 min |
| 4. Forward-chaining walkthrough | Trace the example step by step on the slides: match R1, add the new fact, match R2, state the conclusion. | Eizel Mthana Conqueror | 4 min |
| 5. Audience activity | Learners trace a new case in which the member has an overdue book: which rule applies, which does not, and what can be concluded. Answers are collected and checked together. | Frederic Chandra | 3 min |
| 6. Strength, limitation, and close | One strength (the reasoning can be inspected rule by rule) and one limitation (a rule-based system can only conclude what its facts and rules support); recap of the three steps. | Frederick Bryan The and Gunawan Nicolas Benedictus | 2 min |

**Expected outcome.** By the end of the mini lecture, learners can identify the facts and rule conditions in the scenario, follow how a rule is applied, and state the conclusion for a new case. The slides (Eizel Mthana Conqueror and Ritchard Alexander) carry the diagram and the walkthrough; the activity answer sheet is prepared by the Quality Lead and checked against the test bank. In the activity case the member has an overdue book, so R1 does not fire, “the member may borrow” is never added, and R2 cannot fire: the system concludes nothing, and it cannot conclude “refused” unless a rule says so – which leads directly into segment 6.

